<h3 class="white">YouTube to MP3</h3>
<p>Sometimes YouTube is the only place a recording can be found. This tool turns a YouTube link into MP3s, one per song, ready for Unpack &amp; Convert.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>A song:</strong> one video becomes one MP3.</li>
    <li><strong>A playlist</strong> (the link contains <code>list=</code>): each video becomes its own MP3, numbered in playlist order. Untick any that don't belong.</li>
    <li><strong>A whole album in one video:</strong> choose “A whole album”, check the track titles (one per line, in order), then <strong>Find the tracks</strong>. MetaForge downloads the audio and works out where each song starts: from the video's chapters or the times in its description if it has them, otherwise from the quiet gaps between songs, placed using the track lengths MusicBrainz has for the album.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the cuts:</strong> every start time can be played (a few seconds either side) and corrected before anything is made. Cuts marked <em>Check</em> are the ones to listen to.</li>
    <li><strong>Where they go:</strong> straight into the album folder you choose. The tool never makes a folder of its own, so your folder names stay consistent: make and name the album's folder first (the folder picker has a New folder button). If the folder already has MP3s in it, nothing is made, so two albums never mix. <strong>Continue to Unpack &amp; Convert</strong> carries the folder, artist and album over. That step adds cover art, sets the filing category and writes the album details that MusicBrainz IDs needs. It only renames MP3s, so there's no further loss of quality, unless you tick Apply Loudness Normalization, which re-encodes them.</li>
    <li><strong>Quality:</strong> YouTube's best audio (about 130 kbps) is converted once to MP3 V0, the same as the rest of MetaForge. It can't be better than YouTube's copy.</li>
</ul>
<p class="tool_notes">Note: YouTube changes often. If links stop working, yt-dlp (in the bin folder) needs updating.</p>
